WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:07.090
Humankind loves to look up towards new challenges
and greater aspirations.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:11.950
Whether we’re building immense skyscrapers,
climbing the tallest mountains, or reaching

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.611
for the stars—we never stop striving to
better ourselves.

00:00:15.611 --> 00:00:21.460
But our world has plenty more to explore and
discover even if it means reaching a little

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:22.460
lower.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:23.650
Perhaps a lot lower.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:29.320
Deep beneath the sea, buried far underground,
and even at the very center of the Earth.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:37.989
Let’s take a look at seven of the deepest
places in the world.

00:00:37.989 --> 00:00:43.100
Far beneath the chill waters of the western
Pacific Ocean, the Marianas Trench stretches

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.090
across the seafloor in a realm that light
has never touched.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:53.610
The deepest point—appropriately called “Challenger
Deep”—has been measured at 10,994 kilometres

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:57.160
below sea level, give or take forty meters.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:03.250
The bottom of the trench was seen for the
first time on January 23rd, 1960 by oceanographer

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:09.280
Jacques Piccard along with US Navy Lieutenant
Don Walsh from within a cramped bathyscaphe.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.530
They missed the Challenger Deep by a few meters
but they were still able to observe the floor

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:20.500
of the Marianas Trench, which was noted to
be “light and clear, a waste of snuff-colored

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:21.630
ooze.”

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:26.700
Despite the crushing weight of the water above,
the intrepid explorers were certainly shocked

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:32.690
to find life waiting for them at the bottom,
in the form of “some type of flatfish, resembling

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:36.189
a sole, about one foot long and six inches
across.”

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:40.509
If sixteen thousand pounds of pressure per
square inch can’t dissuade life from finding

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:43.439
a way, perhaps there’s a lesson to be learned
there.

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:49.229
Later expeditions revealed other extreme forms
of life, like giant amoebas measuring ten

00:01:49.229 --> 00:01:51.630
centimeters in diameter.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.450
Before the nightmares set in, it might be
worth noting that the amoebas didn’t exactly

00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:00.799
resemble the single-celled organisms as we
think of them, but more like a sea sponge

00:02:00.799 --> 00:02:02.719
or coral.

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:07.439
Scientists dubbed these creatures “xenophyophores”,
which is science-speak for “they look like

00:02:07.439 --> 00:02:08.439
aliens.”

00:02:08.439 --> 00:02:14.320
The era of discovery continues even to this
day; Hollywood director and science buff James

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:21.550
Cameron performed the deepest solo sub dive
into the Marianas Trench on March 25th, 2012.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:26.550
Using his custom built submersible, he collected
samples of the oozy sediment and even captured

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:31.950
3D video of the entire dive—hoping to gain
a better understanding of the ocean’s depths

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.520
and spur support for future expeditions.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.870
There’s certainly a lot left for us to discover
down there.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:41.310
As long as we don’t anger Cthulhu, we should
be okay.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:47.090
Just a short distance southwest of Johannesburg
in South Africa lies perhaps the greatest

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:52.170
achievement in the world of mining since the
invention of the pickaxe.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:57.120
Miners in the Mponeng Gold Mine have been
hard at work since 1981, sifting the earth

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.290
to produce gold from a vein that averages
less than a meter wide.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:05.520
An interesting dichotomy, when you consider
that the Mponeng Gold Mine is the deepest

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.220
mine in the world—and it’s all thanks
to that relatively tiny golden thread.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:16.510
Most of the work is done between 2.4 and 3.9
kilometers deep, the latter number being the

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:23.700
current world record for mining depth—and
the digging continues, extracting 5,400 metric

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.780
tons of rock daily.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:29.480
Expansion efforts seek to ensure the mine’s
long term viability.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:37.920
Purely a precautionary measure, as in 2012
alone the mine produced around 11,500 kilogrammes

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.070
of gold.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:43.660
That’s $460 million of the golden good stuff.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:48.150
If you’re still undecided on a career path,
perhaps you might consider becoming a miner.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:52.640
But it’s not all fun and games that far
down in the Earth’s crust.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.280
The closer you get to the core of our planet,
the hotter things get in turn.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.580
This is an effect known as the “geothermal
gradient”, and it’s a constant concern

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:04.640
for the intrepid miners of the Mponeng Gold
Mine.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.640
If you were to descend to the deepest point
in the mine, the temperature of the surrounding

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.040
strata would reach temperatures of up to sixty
degrees Celsius.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:19.000
Consider the harsh, unyielding stone surrounding
you—and you’ve got the makings of an oven.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.470
Inhospitable working conditions, for sure.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:26.440
Thanks to a constant stream of slurry ice
pumped through the mine, the temperature is

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:30.470
maintained at a far more bearable thirty degrees
Celsius.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:35.680
Still a sweltering day in the mines, but hey—at
least you won’t end up broiled.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:39.690
Caves have long held a special sort of allure
for explorers.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:43.390
Natural fissures in the terrain which offer
nooks and crannies for those brave enough

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:45.480
to test their shadowy depths.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:51.630
Krubera Cave—located in a particularly porous
area of Western Russia known as the Arabika

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:54.530
Massif—isn’t just any old cave.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:58.860
It bares the unique distinction of being the
deepest cave in the world.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:04.070
It is with no small measure of respect that
some cavers have taken to calling it the “Everest

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:08.790
of the caves”—evoking images of the danger
and excitement that many find in summiting

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:10.800
the tallest mountains.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.090
Krubera Cave was recognized for its record
depth in August of 2004, after a particularly

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:22.000
stoic band of cave explorers followed its
twisting path downward to a total depth of

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.720
2,080 metres.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:29.090
It’s the only natural formation known to
humankind greater than two kilometers deep,

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:34.480
and it even has a reserve of water at the
very bottom known as the “terminal sump”.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.910
That’s as far as the team of cavers could
go with the equipment they had with them.

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:45.970
In 2007, and again in 2012, a Ukranian diver
by the name of Gennady Samokhin climbed down

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:51.680
to the sump with all the necessary equipment
to plunge into the icy water and map the tail

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:52.960
end of the cave.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:58.810
He earned world records with both dives, and
added another ninety-eight meters to the depth

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:00.730
total in the process.

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:05.170
So if you’ve ever fancied a long climb with
a brief swim at the bottom, there’s a bloody

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:08.760
scary idea for you to add to your next vacation.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:14.170
Once you’re through spelunking in Krubera
Cave, head east into Siberia to find the next

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:19.500
stop on our whirlwind tour of the world’s
deepest places: Lake Baikal.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.420
The depth isn’t the only interesting thing
about this crescent shaped reservoir cutting

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:28.210
through the Russian mainland—though it is
both the deepest freshwater lake and the deepest

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.890
continental body of water to be found on our
planet.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:36.570
Besides reaching incredible depths, there’s
plenty of other facts about the lake to get

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:40.620
excited about, especially if you’re a scientist.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:46.490
Geologically, Lake Baikal is the oldest freshwater
lake in the world, and contains nearly 20%

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:49.570
of the world’s unfrozen freshwater reserve.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:54.630
Not to say that it doesn’t freeze over from
time to time—it is Siberia, after all.

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:59.350
The lake and its surrounds are also home to
an incredibly diverse collection of flora

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:05.870
and fauna, with some estimates identifying
around one 1,340 different species of animals

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:10.680
living harmoniously with upwards of 570 species
of plants.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:15.800
A lush, thriving ecosystem to be catalogued
in and around an ancient lake of remarkable

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:19.451
depths—and those eager scientists might
even be able to make it home for dinner.

00:07:19.451 --> 00:07:20.451
Provided that they live somewhere nearby,
of course.

00:07:20.451 --> 00:07:23.680
We’re going to have to bend the rules a
bit for this next one.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:29.440
Though the Bentley Subglacial Trench in Western
Antarctica isn’t technically covered in

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:33.139
water, it is—well, covered in water.

00:07:33.139 --> 00:07:35.230
But not the wet kind.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:40.250
The trench is actually the lowest point on
the Earth not covered by an ocean or other

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:44.850
form of flowing water, sitting at 2.5 kilometers
below sea level.

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:49.550
As you might expect, “subglacial” means
that it is sequestered beneath the frozen

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:51.930
mass of a majestic glacier.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:56.230
Surrounded by many other similar stretches
of ice all across the Antarctic continent.

00:07:56.230 --> 00:08:00.971
This means that the Bentley Subglacial Trench
is actually subterranean, though it’s still

00:08:00.971 --> 00:08:03.280
a point on the Earth’s surface.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:04.919
Just not one you could walk to.

00:08:04.919 --> 00:08:07.080
I told you we’d have to cheat a bit.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:12.090
It was named by the Advisory Committee on
Antarctic Names in 1961, in honor of Charles

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:17.240
R. Bentley and his contributions to Antarctic
research as the chief traverse seismologist

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:21.580
of Bryd Station, between 1957 and 1959.

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:25.750
Seeing as he was the one who led the expedition
which first located the trench and recorded

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:30.110
its depth, it seems an appropriate legacy
for him to leave behind in the snowy wastes.

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:34.469
Who knows—there could be a secluded spot
in Antarctica just waiting for you to slap

00:08:34.469 --> 00:08:36.630
your name on it as well.

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:42.320
When in doubt, start drilling straight down
and don’t stop until the Earth itself rejects

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:43.320
you.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:49.430
Not exactly sensible advice, but someone involved
with the Kola Superdeep Borehole project must

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:52.410
have heard it somewhere and really took it
to heart.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:57.320
During the Cold War, there was a constant
back-and-forth between the United States and

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:02.750
the Soviet Union, each seeking to one-up each
other on the global stage.

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:07.560
Scientific endeavors flew fast and furious,
one of the results being the Soviets’ decision

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:13.230
to set a drill into the ground of the Kola
Peninsula and dig as deep as possible.

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:18.750
There were certainly valid scientific reasons
for the project, but it almost sounds as if

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:21.390
they were just waiting for an excuse to do
so.

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:27.650
The drilling began in 1970 and didn’t stop
until 1994, producing what we call today the

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:34.640
Kola Superdeep Borehole, the deepest hole
ever drilled to date and the deepest artificial

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:40.120
point in the world, reaching a mind-boggling
twelve kilometers into the crust of our planet.

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:43.460
That’s deeper than even the Challenger Deep.

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:48.480
In the end, the borehole encountered the same
issue as the miners in the Mponeng Gold Mine—things

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:51.730
get really hot that deep underground.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:57.800
Temperatures at the end of the shaft climbed
to around 180 degrees Celsius—no, not Fahrenheit,

00:09:57.800 --> 00:09:58.990
but Celsius.

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:04.100
That’s a brain-melting 356 Fahrenheit.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:09.360
The equipment simply couldn’t continue to
function in those conditions, and so the Kola

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:12.430
Superdeep Borehole had reached its end.

00:10:12.430 --> 00:10:18.190
But rumors continue to swirl about what exactly
brought about the conclusion of the drilling.

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:23.810
There are campfire ghost stories about microphones
lowered into the shaft, which recorded the

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:25.890
sounds of tortured screams.

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:29.030
What’s really going on at the center of
the Earth?

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:30.990
Well let me tell you…

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:37.190
At the very center of our planet lies a sphere
of solid iron—at least, as best we can tell

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:38.290
with current data.

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:44.000
Deeper than the molten mantle and liquid metal
outer core, the inner core seems to be formed

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:47.649
of a massive, non-uniform ball of iron.

00:10:47.649 --> 00:10:52.440
It is this core, and the extreme heat as a
result of several factors working in tandem,

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:55.060
that serves to heat the planet from within.

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:59.380
The same heat which wards off any attempts
to burrow through the crust; it’s as if

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:02.160
the underworld is feverously trying keep us
at bay.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:04.930
There must be something going on down there.

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:09.160
The estimated temperature at the boundary
between the molten metal of the outer core

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:15.149
and the solid inner core is around 5,600 degrees
Kelvin.

00:11:15.149 --> 00:11:22.670
To put that in some perspective, the temperature
of our Sun’s surface is 5,800 degrees Kelvin—only

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:27.800
two hundred more, some predict that the Earth’s
core might even be hotter than the surface

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:29.240
of the sun.

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:34.130
Thanks to pressure, friction, and radioactive
elements, the Earth essentially has a miniature

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:36.370
sun burning at its very heart.

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:41.290
Perhaps we will never get to see the very
core of the Earth with our own eyes, but we

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:42.420
can dream.

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:47.970
And every crevice, borehole, or trench we
discover can bring us that much closer to

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:50.840
understanding the unsearchable depths beneath
us.

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:56.899
And no matter how deep we go, it can only
get warmer and brighter once the darkest places

00:11:56.899 --> 00:12:18.590
are behind us.

